[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Draw the boundary between defining semantics and this system's semantics

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SEMANTICS-DEFINITION-BOUNDARY
TITLE: Draw the boundary between defining semantics and this system's semantics
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Two layers, framed by the operator on 2026-08-25. The definition layer is how
the semantics of any model are defined: a grammar; the state transitions over
its fields; the gates on those transitions; the triggers that fire them; the
flows that hit them; where validators wire in and which hook point each one
belongs to; which validations run on CRUD-like operations versus commit and
merge operations; and how a grammar maps to structured CLI arguments. The
instance layer is this design system's particular semantics: fingerprints,
contracts, the ripple, DEPTH, signing authority.

CRUD and git operations blur the line. They are assumed to be part of the
semantic vocabulary and need a placement in the definition layer.

TODO: define that boundary in docs/spec where it belongs, and link code to
the layer it belongs to with parent and child relations against the boundary
nodes, so an implementation of semantics is never mistaken for the definition
of semantics.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
SPIKE-EXECUTABLE-RULES answered at the instance layer while the operator was
asking at the definition layer. The two are easy to conflate because the
instance is the only worked example, and conflating them is how a meta-model
ends up shaped by one implementation's preferences.
<<<
